 a big error and now not  only black is a clear pawn  up but we are attacking the knight three  times and get more advantage  from this position  well guess what just one move  and blacks all dream shattered  and when in fact my opponent did played  the move queen to a4 check  suddenly to my surprise i realize  how crafty this trap is  came over guys because by force  white is winning a piece as if you  continue with  bishop to d7 then after queen captures  d4  white emerged with the whole knight up  and the other move knight to c6  completely fails  because of queen captures g4 and once  again  this time around white image with a  whole bishop  so this is one of the crafty trap in the  3c3 alpine  where instead of bishop to e2 here  you can literally surprise your opponent  especially in the quick game  with knight b to d2 and  punish your greedy opponent just within  the opening stage  the second trap occurs in the elephant  gambit  where the opening motors are as follow  e4 e5 knight to f3  and now rather than defending the e5  square black played this  tactical idea namely d5  okay by far the most popular choice is  he captures d5  and here black continued with e4  attacking the knight theory continue  with  queen to e2 pinning down that pawn  and attacking on e4 so black defended  with knight to f6 and now white put more  pressure  on the e pawn with d3  well now comes the move which sets up a  very cunning trap  and that is bishop to b4 check  here there are two popular choices one  is  c3 and second is bishop to d2  now if your opponent plays the move c3  then after  castle black get the huge initiative  and i have attached a sample game in the  pgn  so you can check it out but our main  trap occurs  when white plays the move bishop to d2  which looks more logical in this  position  idea is very obvious that if black took  this bishop  then after knight b to d2 white gain  more pressure  on the e4 spot  okay black castles at this point  and again if white plays the move d  captures e4  then after rook to e8 black get  huge attack especially on the e  file where white king and a queen  sitting there  so most of the white player has  considered the move  bishop captures b4 not only grabbing a  piece  but attacking that rook and in fact  few strong games has gone into this  direction  which i am afraid a completely wrong  idea  here white has straight away fallen into  the black trappy territory  and the trappy business start with this  very obvious move  he captures f3 attacking the queen  queen captures f3 and although  white has a bishop pair and few pawns he  is completely  losing from this position because of  this wonderful check  obviously if the king moves then that  clearly justify  the black's loss of pawn but you may ask  how about bishop to e2 isn't that  solve all white problem  nope not at all because just one  bishop to g4  and suddenly white can resign the game  as there is no way to defend on the e2  square  so this is one of the high profile trap  exist  in the elephant gambit which you  definitely want to consider  in your bloodscapes  the third high profile trap occurs after  the following  opening more order c4  e5 knight to c3  knight to c6 knight to f3  and now black plays the reverse grand  prix attack setup  starting with f5  well obviously white shouldn't allow the  space  and accordingly d4 is the best response  to whom black should gain more space on  the king's side  with e4  here the old theory suggests that bite  should go with this  knight maneuvering however  the modern approach is to play bishop to  g5  so getting rid of that bishop before we  close with  knight to d2  okay black continue with knight to f6  and now here white should play the moon  knight to d2  which if you look at first time looks  like a blunder  because can't black can simply take this  pawn  well of course he can but the big point  is  after knight captures e4 white  gained his material and this position  slightly  favors the white cam  but as happened many times sometimes  people don't know the theory  and there are few high profile games  continue with  d5 counter-attacking the black knight  and after this yup you guessed it  white has straight away fallen into the  black  trapezoid for a moment  it doesn't look like there is any trap  in this position  because after pawn takes f3 d takes c6  and pawn takes g2 yes  white cannot just simply take on g2  because after b capture c6  black emerged with an extra pawn  but he can continue with this  intermediate move  c captures d7 comes with a check  so that no matter however black  recapture in the next turn he can  capture on g2  and the remaining position looks quite  okay  well that will be the case if black  capture with queen or bishop  but to white surprise  black took the pawn with the knight  and now you see the beautiful idea  yes indeed black queen is hanging  but if white take the queen then  black can take the rook and promote a  new queen  so that is right out of the equation  and the base reply bishop captures g2  completely fails  because after simple queen captures g5  if you count the material black emerge  with a clear piece advantage  that's it guys i hope you enjoy and  learn this  three high profile mini traps  well thank you for watching this video  feel free to like subscribe and comment  and i will meet you in my next episode  very soon  bye and take care

